            <ArticleTitle>Pseudomonas osteomyelitis of the proximal humerus after arthroscopic rotator cuff repair.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>685-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3944/AOTT.2014.13.0015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 59-year-old male patient was operated arthroscopically due to a rotator cuff tear. An early postoperative Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection was treated with early arthroscopic debridement and antibiotic therapy. The patient was lost to follow-up and presented to our clinic with Pseudomonas aeruginosa osteomyelitis after two years. Debridement was again performed and antibiotic-impregnated cement beads were filled into the cavity and taken out 6 weeks postoperatively. No findings of infection were observed at the patient's 2nd year follow-up. To our knowledge, this is the first case of Pseudomonas aeruginosa osteomyelitis of the shoulder after arthroscopic rotator cuff repair.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
